# So the project artefact will be an LLM security code corrector/reviewer.
# Questions to answer:
# 1. How can LLMs be used to identify security vulnerabilities in source code?
# 2. How can LLMs be integrated into existing code review processes to enhance security?
# 3. What are the limitations and challenges of using LLMs for secure code review, and how can they be addressed?
# 4. How can the performance of LLM-based secure code review be evaluated and compared to traditional static analysis tools?
# 5. Static analysis tools that I can compare to are Bandit, Semgrep and Veracode (commercial, need to check I can get access).
# 6. Compare the data between my LLM-based secure code review and traditional static analysis.
# Then compare my data with the data from the research papers.
# Edit: Maybe at the start of use, there is an option to say what your aim of execution is.
# 2. Map API endpoints against authorisation frameworks to flag missing access
#    control checks.
# Identifies vulnerabilities within the code.
# The LLM, much like a normal code corrector, will notify of a problem and
# suggest a fix.
# The LLM must then explain why. Second, at every mistake, explain that the root
# cause could be deeper at an infrastructure level; this is something to think
# about, including what the scanner can do.
# Tools to use (could change): DeepSeek, Python, Ollama. Their respective
# libraries and further development may be needed.
# References:
# - https://arxiv.org/abs/2407.12241
# - https://arxiv.org/html/2401.16310v5
# - https://arxiv.org/abs/2308.14434
# - https://pdf.sciencedirectassets.com/271629/1-s2.0-S0164121223X00065/
#   1-s2.0-S0164121223001474/main.pdf
# LLM-Based Secure Code Reviewer: Develop and evaluate a secure code-review
# system that uses Large Language Models (LLMs) to identify potential security
# vulnerabilities and insecure programming practices in source code. The project
# can investigate the accuracy, limitations, and practical usefulness of
# LLM-based code review compared with traditional static analysis tools.

# Start of production
# 1. Needs to read what is being said / needs to read when prompted - this is a separate file
# 2. Needs to connect what is being said to the LLM, which then sends out the output
# 3. Needs to log what's happening, including failures on either the LLM side or the reading side
# 4. Needs to be able to ask on the infrastructure side
# 5. Main file will keep deployment.


Date - 26/10/2026, session 1

Done - Installed and tested the LLMs that will be used. They need more tinkering to be accurate, as Qwen gave a fix that would not work on SQLite (it used the %s placeholder but SQLite needs ?), so I need to handle that somewhere. The test itself was successful. d3 gave a high score for the SQL injection example, and Qwen gave an explanation and a fix when prompted separately. The two are not linked together yet.

Next - Put the prompts in prompts.py in a JSON format and test that it works. Then feed them through llm_client.py to check they reach the LLMs properly. d3 should return a score that is turned into a yes or no using a threshold. If it is yes, the code should be sent to Qwen 2.5 to explain the vulnerability.

Decisions - Paid or cloud-hosted LLMs cost money or send code off my machine, and DeepSeek reasoning models are likely to be too slow on a machine with no dedicated GPU and 16GB RAM. As such d3 and Qwen 2.5 will be used. Can write about this in the report. These two LLMs will be used in conjunction with each other. d3 will decide whether it's a vulnerability. Flagged code will then be sent to Qwen, which will decide whether it agrees and explain the decision.


Date - 26/10/2026, session 2

Done
- Built the first working prototype. d3 scores whether a piece of code contains a given vulnerability and Qwen 2.5 Coder 7B explains it and suggests a fix. Both are called from my own code through Ollama and return structured results.
- Wrote a smoke test using four hand-written samples. All four matched my expectations at a threshold of 0.5.
- d3 scores: vulnerable SQL 0.9998, safe parameterised SQL 0.0025, safe hardcoded SQL 0.0045, subprocess sample 0.276 (asked the SQL injection question).
- The test reports which sample failed.

Problems found
- Qwen called the subprocess flaw SQL injection when it is command injection. It was told a scanner suspected SQL injection and went along with it.
- Qwen's fix for the subprocess sample came back on one line and is not valid Python.
- Qwen used the %s placeholder in its SQL fix, which does not work with SQLite (needs ?). This has now happened twice.
- The subprocess sample scored much higher than the safe samples on a SQL question. d3 may be reacting to user input being built into an executed string in general. It is only one sample so it needs more testing.
- Pass or fail only checks d3 against my expectation, so Qwen can be wrong and the run still passes.

Next (each task has a done-when)
1. Time each model call. Done when the experiment log has seconds per sample for each stage.
2. Run each sample three times. Done when the log shows whether d3 scores and Qwen answers change between runs.
3. Run Bandit and Semgrep on the same four samples. Done when their results sit next to d3's in the log.
4. Add Qwen checks: does the fix parse as Python and does Qwen name the right weakness. Done when failures from Qwen show up separately in the report.
5. Move the prompts into a JSON format and load them through llm_client.py. Done when the d3 questions load from the file and a score comes back.
6. Link the two stages in reviewer.py so only flagged code reaches Qwen. Done when a safe sample never gets sent to Qwen.

Decisions
- Threshold set at 0.5 for now. To be tuned later using more samples.
- The smoke test sends every sample to Qwen, including ones d3 calls safe, to see whether Qwen wrongly agrees something is vulnerable. The real pipeline will only send flagged code.
- Results are stored as one record per sample (name, score, verdict, passed).

Open questions
- Where should the model names live so I can swap models for comparison?
- How should the d3 and Qwen prompts be worded, and how should user input change them?
- What should happen if the user does not say which vulnerabilities to check?